<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">D. F. Malan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Daniel François Malan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">D. F. Malan was a South African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Reformed minister, newspaper editor and politician who served as prime minister from 1948 to 1954. Leading the Reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, he won the 1948 election on the platform of apartheid and began building it into law with the Population Registration Act, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a> and other measures. He retired in 1954 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-g-strijdom">J. G. Strijdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/d-f-malan.jpg" alt="D. F. Malan" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suidpunt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DFMalanPortret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1948 <b>D. F. Malan</b>’s Reunited National Party won power and began apartheid with laws such as the Population Registration Act and the Group Areas Act of 1950.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the election of May 1948 the National Party of <b>D. F. Malan</b>, speaking for Afrikaner nationalism and for white fears of a growing Black urban population, won a majority of seats, though not of votes, on the programme of apartheid, “separateness”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Riebeek West, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in Stellenbosch, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.35,18.87&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Riebeek West on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5032 3374 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of D. F. Malan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5482 3644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5482 3644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.35,18.87&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
